  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSummer continues to want to hold on across the region as\nlow-level southerly flow returns today, throughout this \nweekend, and to start next week. Widespread low to mid 90s are \nexpected today with mid to upper 90s by Saturday. A cold front's\nplacement will determine who sees upper 80s or mid to upper 90s\non Saturday, with northern zones having the best chance to be a\ntouch cooler. However, by Sunday and Monday, everyone is back to\nhot temperatures. Mid to upper 90s are expected, with 100s\nlikely making their presence felt again closer to the KS/OK \nborder area. NBM probabilities of MaxTs >100 is 40-70% in areas\nsouth of Route 50 to the Oklahoma border. Beyond Monday, a cold\nfront sweeps through, knocking temperatures down across the \nregion. Current guidance suggests this stretch will be more \nprolonged through next week.\n\nPrecipitation chances are minimal for parts of the CWA today,\nwith only slight chance in far northern and eastern counties\nthis evening and overnight. CAMs do attempt thunderstorm\ndevelopment across northern Kansas given a fleeting influence\nfrom a shortwave trough. Cannot entirely rule out some activity\nnearing I-70 into this evening given this, but overall chances \nare low as well as any severe weather. Sunday will feature a \ngreater chance at thunderstorms along a weak cold front that \nwill position itself west to east across Kansas. Moisture along \nthe boundary, fostering MUCAPE around 2000-3000 J/kg, would \nallow at least some isolated thunderstorm potential. PWATs \naround 1.3\" and steep low-level lapse rates would promote wet \nmicroburst potential. Locally heavy rainfall would also be a \npotential given the very moist column. Bulk shear 30-40 knots \nwould suggest some at least semi-organization of multi-cell \nclusters, and perhaps a transient supercell structure, so\nisolated instances of hail would also be possible. The favored \narea for any isolated severe thunderstorm chances would be east \nof Route 283, where POPs are 20-50% in the latest forecast. \nAdditional waves sweeping in the from the west and boundary \nplacement will maintain elevated POPs in the forecast through \nnext week.",
